<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christophe Plantin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1520–<a href="/years/1589">1589</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christophe Plantin was a French-born printer and publisher who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1549 and built one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> businesses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His press, the Golden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">Compasses</a>, produced the eight-volume Polyglot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> for Philip II between 1568 and 1573, besides scientific, humanist and liturgical works. His workshop, run by his Moretus heirs for three centuries, is now the Plantin-Moretus Museum, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christophe-plantin.jpg" alt="Christophe Plantin" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Christoffel_Plantin_-_WGA20356.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Plantin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, probably in Saint-Avertin, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, to a family that was not wealthy; his mother died while he was young. He apprenticed as a bookbinder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, where he also married. In 1545 he and his wife, Joanna Rivière, set up shop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, but after three years they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, where he became a free citizen and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of St Luke, the guild of painters, sculptors, engravers and printers. His bookbinding brought him into contact with nobility and wealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A wound to his arm, received when he was mistakenly attacked while delivering a prestigious commission, ended his work as a bookbinder and turned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. By 1555 he had his own print shop; the first book he is known to have printed was La Institutione di una fanciulla nata nobilmente, by Giovanni Michele Bruto.</p><p>In 1561 the Spanish governor ordered a raid on his workshop on suspicion of heretical works, and in 1562 his presses and goods were seized and sold after his workmen printed a heretical pamphlet while he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. With the help of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> merchants he re-established the business and acquired a house in the Hoogstraat named De Gulden Passer, the Golden Compasses. His printer's mark showed compasses held by a hand from the clouds, with the motto Labore et <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Constantia</a>.</p><p>His most important work was the Biblia Regia, or Plantin Polyglot, with parallel texts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. Philip II of Spain encouraged it and sent Benito Arias Montano to lead the editing. Plantin mortgaged his business to pay for it, and it took thirteen presses and fifty-three men and earned little profit. In return Philip granted him the privilege of printing Roman Catholic liturgical books for the states he ruled and the title Architypographus Regii. By 1575 the firm had more than 20 presses and 73 workmen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the Spanish sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in November 1576 he paid a heavy ransom to protect his works, and in 1583 he moved temporarily to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, leaving the Antwerp business to his sons-in-law Jan Moretus and Frans van Ravelinghen. He returned after the city surrendered in 1585 and worked there until his death on 1 July 1589. The Moretus family left the workshop untouched, and in 1877 the city of Antwerp opened it as the Musée Plantin. The Christophe Plantin Prize was created in his memory in 1968.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christophe Plantin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 1560s Antwerp had around 100,000 inhabitants and was the commercial and financial capital of northern Europe, and a centre of printing where <b>Christophe Plantin</b> ran one of the largest presses of the age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Avertin, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.37,0.73&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Saint-Avertin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Christophe Plantin"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5042 6071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5042 6071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.37,0.73&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
